These tools are about performance optimization, but they are only useful when you have a real problem to solve.
A good rule is:
first write clear code
then optimize only if profiling or a real bottleneck shows you need it
memo


memo is a higher-order component that tells React:
"If the props for this component did not change, do not re-render it just because its parent re-rendered."
import { memo } from 'react';

const Counter = memo(function Counter({ initialCount }: { initialCount: number }) {
  console.log('Counter rendered');

  return <div>Count: {initialCount}</div>;
});

export default function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount((prev) => prev + 1)}>Update parent</button>
      <Counter initialCount={0} />
    </>
  );
}

In this example, the parent state changes, but the Counter prop value stays the same. Because the props did not change, React can skip re-rendering the child component.
This is useful when the child is expensive to render and the input props are unchanged.
Before using memo, check whether the component structure can be improved.
Sometimes the better solution is to move the state lower in the tree or split the component into smaller pieces, instead of adding optimization too early.
useCallback


useCallback is used to keep a function reference stable across renders.
This matters when a child component is wrapped in memo and receives a function as a prop.
Without useCallback, the function is recreated every render, so React sees a new prop value and may re-render the child.
