This tutorial focuses on one important hook: useEffect.
useEffect does


useEffect runs after React has rendered the component and committed the DOM updates.
That means it is not part of the render itself. It runs after the UI has been painted, which makes it a good place for:
fetching data
subscribing to events
starting timers
cleaning up resources
useEffect runs:
once after the initial render
again whenever one of its dependencies changes




import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export default function TimerExample() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [userId, setUserId] = useState(1);

  useEffect(() => {
    console.log('Effect runs after render for user:', userId);

    const timer = window.setInterval(() => {
      setCount((prev) => prev + 1);
    }, 1000);

    return () => {
      console.log('Cleanup runs before the next effect or before unmount');
      window.clearInterval(timer);
    };
  }, [userId]);

  return (
    <>
      <p>User: {userId}</p>
      <p>Seconds: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setUserId((prev) => prev + 1)}>Next user</button>
    </>
  );
}

On the first render, the effect runs after the component is mounted.
It creates a timer and starts counting.
When userId changes, React runs the cleanup function from the previous effect.
Then React runs the new effect for the updated value.
When the component unmounts, the cleanup still runs one final time.
If the effect returns a function, that function is treated as cleanup.
React runs cleanup:
before the next effect runs
before the component is removed from the DOM
This is important because cleanup is where we usually stop timers, unsubscribe from listeners, and release resources.
useEffect(() => {
  const subscription = someEventSource.subscribe();

  return () => {
    subscription.unsubscribe();
  };
}, [someValue]);

In short:
effect = setup work
cleanup = teardown work
